{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive, strategic imperative to maximize the rate of error elimination, not as an inference to probable truth.","counterconditions":["If the goal of science shifts from error elimination to predictive reliability for engineering safety, this strategic defense collapses without an inductive bridge.","If 'severity' of tests cannot be defined independently of the theory being tested, the metric for 'better-corroborated' becomes circular."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reframing scientific action as a game of 'maximal risk exposure': choosing the theory that has survived the most severe tests is equivalent to choosing the weapon with the highest known durability in battle, not because it is 'magically immune' to future damage (induction), but because it is the only rational bet for surviving the next immediate engagement given the goal of eliminating error. The 'cost' is accepting that this preference offers no guarantee of future success or proximity to truth, only the best available method for exposing falsity.","optional_refs":["1a76afe6cb3e70b8a8b152c1f41b2ecae41c069eb31312939cf041481ed04807"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference smuggles induction back in because the concept of 'severe testing' implicitly relies on the uniformity of nature: assuming that conditions which falsified rivals in the past will remain relevant or similar in the future.","counterconditions":["If 'severity' can be defined purely logically (e.g., logical content) without reference to empirical regularities, the inductive smuggling charge fails.","If the preference is viewed solely as a psychological habit of scientists rather than a normative rule, the logical contradiction dissolves."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Deconstructing 'Severity': A test is only 'severe' if the outcome was improbable given the background knowledge. This background knowledge itself rests on the assumption that natural regularities observed previously hold now. Therefore, preferring a theory based on past severe tests assumes the future will resemble the past regarding the relevance of those tests, which is the very principle of induction Popper rejected. The 'cost' of accepting this is admitting Popper's system is not purely deductive and requires a minimal, perhaps tacit, inductive postulate to function in practice.","optional_refs":["227f1deae23bf083d99c6563c1f5e5196631d4667ea52aa00cf995e64cee49fd"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma is a category error: Popper's 'preference' is not an epistemic judgment about the world, but an aesthetic or methodological commitment to 'boldness,' making the question of defensibility irrelevant to truth-tracking.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly linked corroboration to verisimilitude (closeness to truth), this aesthetic reduction contradicts his later writings.","If acting on a theory (e.g., building a bridge) requires a belief in its reliability, an aesthetic preference is insufficient for practical application."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Recasting Corroboration as Aesthetic: Just as a critic prefers a complex symphony over a simple tune not because it is 'truer' but because it offers more opportunities for failure (and thus greater achievement if it succeeds), scientists prefer bold theories for their informational content. The preference is defensible as a commitment to high-content inquiry, not as a strategy for finding true descriptions of reality. The 'cost' is abandoning the idea that science progressively approaches truth (verisimilitude) in any meaningful sense, reducing it to an endless generation of complex falsehoods.","optional_refs":["6b8f9c6828d8c70b6a4638b762b9c9889306081ca7479f1a68a615433ddaa93b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}